## Summary

In Safari and other WebKit browsers (JavaScriptCore), the `removeBySelector` step takes far longer than in Chrome. It accounts for most of `parse()` time on long pages. The cause is `PARTIAL_SELECTORS_REGEX`: three of the ~550 `PARTIAL_SELECTORS` patterns use a lookbehind (`(?<!main-)access-wall`, the `logo` pattern, `(?<!h[1-6]-)related`). JavaScriptCore can't JIT-compile a regex that contains a lookbehind. The whole combined alternation then runs in its interpreter, about 25–60× slower. V8 is unaffected.

## Measurements

These are from defuddle 0.19.5 in WKWebView (macOS 14.8, Safari 26.2 engine), on live pages. `parse()` ran on a `cloneNode(true)` copy, and each figure is the median of 3 runs:

| Page | `removeBySelector` | whole `parse()` |
|---|---|---|
| Quanta Magazine article | 153 ms | 170 ms |
| Wikipedia, Moons of Jupiter | 448 ms | 540 ms |
| Wikipedia, Fourier transform | 1,882 ms | 2,200 ms |

The same pages in Chrome spend 3–5 ms in `removeBySelector`.

On Moons of Jupiter, splitting that step up showed: the exact-selector `querySelectorAll` takes 17 ms, the attribute strings 2 ms and the partial-selector regex tests **434 ms**. That is 3,512 strings and 86 KB of class/attribute text.

## Reduced case

Run this in Safari's console and in Chrome's:

```js
// 540 literal alternatives, like PARTIAL_SELECTORS, tested against 4,000 class strings.
const words = Array.from({ length: 540 }, (_, i) => 'token' + i.toString(36) + '-x');
const strings = Array.from({ length: 4000 }, (_, i) => `mw-content-ltr item-${i} vector-body`);
const time = (re) => {
  const t = performance.now();
  for (let k = 0; k < 3; k++) for (const s of strings) re.test(s);
  return Math.round((performance.now() - t) / 3);
};
console.log({
  'no lookbehind (ms)': time(new RegExp(words.join('|'), 'i')),
  'plus one lookbehind (ms)': time(new RegExp([...words, '(?<!main-)access-wall'].join('|'), 'i')),
  'plus one lookahead (ms)': time(new RegExp([...words, 'access-wall(?!-main)'].join('|'), 'i')),
});
```

WebKit gives `{ no lookbehind: 10, plus one lookbehind: 631, plus one lookahead: 4 }`. V8 (Node 22) gives `{ 3, 1, 1 }`.

## Suggested fix

Compile the lookbehind patterns into a regex of their own and test both. A string matches the alternation exactly when it matches one of the two parts, so the results don't change. I'll open a PR that does this for `PARTIAL_SELECTORS_REGEX` and `PARTIAL_SELECTORS_ANCHORED_REGEX`, with a test that checks both agree with the combined regex. With it, `removeBySelector` drops to 13 / 48 / 154 ms on the three pages above. `parse()` drops to 29 / 158 / 620 ms, with byte-identical `content`.

On `main` at `556d74e8`, heading cleanup replaces inline nodes with `textContent`. A heading containing two authored MathML expressions becomes concatenated presentation/TeX text, and its author link is lost. Cleanup also treats a standardized footnote link (`sup[id="fnref:1"] > a[href="#fn:1"]`) as a heading permalink, leaving an orphaned backlink or an empty marker.

Minimal Node reproduction (normal extraction, enough surrounding prose to avoid retry):

```js
import { parseHTML } from 'linkedom';
import { Defuddle } from 'defuddle/node';

const math = '<math><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^2</annotation></semantics></math>';
const prose = '<p>' + 'This article explains the measurements and the conditions used for each observation. '.repeat(30) + '</p>';
const ref = '<sup class="footnote-reference"><a role="doc-noteref" href="#measurement-note">1</a></sup>';
const { document } = parseHTML(`<html><head><title>Heading content</title></head><body><article>${prose}
<h2><span><a href="#results">#</a>Results ${math} and ${math}${ref} with <a href="/method#details">the method</a></span></h2>
<p>The explanation applies to this observation too${ref}.</p>
<div class="footnotes"><ol><li id="measurement-note"><p>Measurement conditions.</p></li></ol></div>
</article></body></html>`);
const result = await Defuddle(document, 'https://example.com/article', { useAsync: false, separateMarkdown: true });
console.log(result.content, result.contentMarkdown);
```

Expected: two separate inline expressions, the author link, and a working heading footnote reference; both return links target actual references. Remove the real `#results` permalink.

Actual: math is flattened (for example, `x2x^2 and x2x^2`), the author link is lost, and the heading footnote reference is removed. The ordinary paragraph reference survives. Reproduces with Linkedom and JSDOM.

This concerns heading normalization, not caption normalization, footnote-body collection, or general MathML conversion. Existing heading hierarchy/section-selection reports concern different behavior.

## Problem

On a Reddit listing page such as https://www.reddit.com/, the synchronous extractor returns the body of the first post and silently omits the other loaded posts. This makes feed summaries incomplete even when the content is already present in the DOM.

Tested with Defuddle **0.19.3**, using both browser DOM extraction in Chrome and the standalone reproduction below. No authentication, network requests, shadow DOM, or extension-specific preprocessing are needed for the minimal reproduction.

## Minimal reproduction

Install `defuddle@0.19.3` and `jsdom@27.0.1`, then run this as an ES module with Node.js 22.12+:

```js
import { JSDOM } from 'jsdom';
import { Defuddle } from 'defuddle/node';

const url = 'https://www.reddit.com/';
const dom = new JSDOM(`<!doctype html>
<title>Reddit feed</title>
<main>
  <shreddit-post post-title="First post" author="example_a">
    <div slot="text-body"><p>FIRST_POST_SENTINEL: First post body.</p></div>
  </shreddit-post>
  <shreddit-post post-title="Second post" author="example_b">
    <div slot="text-body"><p>SECOND_POST_SENTINEL: Second post body.</p></div>
  </shreddit-post>
</main>`, { url });

try {
  const result = await Defuddle(dom.window.document, url, {
    markdown: true,
    useAsync: false,
  });
  console.log(result.content);
  const text = result.content.replace(/\\/g, '');
  console.log({
    firstPost: text.includes('FIRST_POST_SENTINEL'),
    secondPost: text.includes('SECOND_POST_SENTINEL'),
  });
} finally {
  dom.window.close();
}
```

Actual result:

```js
{ firstPost: true, secondPost: false }
```

Expected: both post bodies are retained when extracting the listing, or the single-post extractor declines this page so the generic pipeline can handle it.

## Likely cause

In [RedditExtractor](https://github.com/kepano/defuddle/blob/main/src/extractors/reddit.ts), the constructor selects the first `shreddit-post`, and `canExtract()` accepts any page containing such an element (or an old-Reddit listing item). It does not distinguish a listing from an individual post page. The extraction then uses that first post's body.

The same applicability check is present in the current main branch at the time of filing. The existing comments-page check is used for async extraction, but does not guard synchronous `canExtract()`.

Setting `contentSelector: 'main'` or relaxing the general cleanup options does not resolve this, because the site-specific extractor is selected before the generic content-selection pipeline.

## Browser cross-check

On a loaded Reddit homepage, an experimental local bypass of the site-specific extractor allowed the generic pipeline, with less aggressive cleanup, to retain all 28 checked post links and titles. This is supporting evidence that the immediate problem is extractor selection, rather than unavailable page content; it is not a claim that the generic default settings preserve every feed detail.

Would it make sense to restrict the single-post extractor to individual post pages and let listing pages fall through to the generic pipeline? A regression test with multiple posts on a listing, alongside existing single-post/comment tests, could protect this behavior.

I am using defuddle and specifically the `YoutubeExtractor` functionalities for a project. I wish for the possibility to prevent the grouping of transcript segments. I find the others could find this functionality beneficial.

If it sounds okay, I'm willing to create a PR for this.
Specifically, the implementation I thought of would be something like:

#### type.ts
```
interface DefuddleOptions {
  // ...existing options

  // Prevent YoutubeExtractor to group transcript segments
  // Default to false
  preserveTranscriptSegments?: boolean;
}
```

or, being a YouTube specific option:
```
export interface DefuddleOptions {
  // ...existing options

  extractors?: {
    youtube?: {
      // Prevent YoutubeExtractor to group transcript segments
      // Default to false
      preserveTranscriptSegments?: boolean;
    };
  };
}
```

#### defuddle.ts
```
const extractor = ExtractorRegistry.findPreferredAsyncExtractor(this.doc, url, schemaOrgData, this.options.extractors);
```

#### youtube.ts
```
export class YoutubeExtractor extends BaseExtractor {
  private preserveTranscriptSegments: boolean;

  constructor(document: Document, url: string, schemaOrgData?: any, options?: { preserveTranscriptSegments?: boolean }) {
    super(document, url, schemaOrgData);
    this.videoElement = document.querySelector('video');
    this.schemaOrgData = schemaOrgData;
    this.preserveTranscriptSegments = options?.preserveTranscriptSegments ?? false;
  }
  // ...
}
```

```
private groupTranscriptSegments(segments: { start: number; text: string }[]): { start: number; text: string; speakerChange: boolean; speaker?: number }[] {
  if (segments.length === 0) return [];

  if (this.preserveTranscriptSegments) {
    return segments.map(seg => ({
      start: seg.start,
      text: seg.text,
      speakerChange: false,
    }));
  }

  // ...existing logic
}
```

Thank you for your time.
